  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe will use a three-stage approach combining linear programming-based search with gradient descent and coordinate-wise optimization. The central idea is to iteratively improve the sequence by finding directions that decrease the AC1 upper bound, using the current maximum autocorrelation as an optimizer's constraint. We start from the parent sequence (length 384) and consider other lengths from 256 and 512. The algorithm balances exploration through random initialization with exploitation via direction-finding LP and gradient projection. We expect this approach to yield significantly better than the parent's 1.5095 by finding sequences with well-controlled, reduced autocorrelation.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nVariables: Sequence coefficients f = [f0, f1, ..., f_{n-1}] with f_i \u2208 \u211d\u207a (nonnegative). Normalization: Sum(f) \u2265 \u03b4 (0.01, taken care by evaluator). Objective: Minimize score = (2 * n * max(f*f)) / (sum(f))\u00b2. Constraints: f_i \u2208 [0, M] (automatically enforced by evaluator). We formulate an LP problem at each iteration: Maximize sum(g) subject to ||f*g||_\u221e \u2264 M, with g \u2265 0. This helps find valid search directions that maintain feasibility and reduce the score.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n- Start with parent sequence + initialization methods (sine, cosine, multi-frequency).\n- For each candidate sequence, run:\n   1. Iterative LP-guided search: Find optimal g via LP, project to valid sequence, repeat until score stabilizes or time expires.\n   2. Gradient polishing: Use sparse convolution and gradient descent with momentum to refine the solution.\n   3. Coordinate-wise optimization: Try small perturbations to coordinates that affect the maximum convolution value.\n- Score various lengths (256, 384, 512). Return best found.\n\n   Helper functions:\n   - `_get_parent()`: Return parent sequence.\n   - `_conv_fft(a)`: Fast convolution via FFT of length.\n   - `_score(a)`: Any of the evaluator or custom fft-based score.\n   - `_build_A(f, n)`: Build the convolution constraint matrix.\n   - `_lp_solve(f, n, M)`: Solve the LP optimization.\n   - `_line_search(f, g_dir, n...)`: Line search in new direction.\n   - `_lp_loop(f0, n...)`: Iterative LP-guided improvement.\n   - `_subgrad_polish(f0, n...)`: Subgradient steps for speed.\n   - `_coord_polish(f0, n...)`: Local coordinate optimization.\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n- Base length fixed at 384 unless searching other lengths.\n- Initialization schemes: Parent, cosine, random.\n- Optimization method combinations: LP + polishing/gradients.\n- Parameters:\n   - Initial magnitudes: Square-root/negate component-wise.\n   - Time allocation: \n     - Phase1: LP-guided search for 55% of budget.\n     - Phase2: Subgradient polish for 15%.\n     - Phase3: Other lengths for 20%.\n     - Phase4: Final cleanup for 10%.\n   - Score threshold: 1.503 to trigger continuation.\n   - Stalling threshold: Max 60 stall increments.\n   - Step factors: 0.1-0.3 for perturbations.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Total budget: 1300 seconds (22 mins).\n- Time monitoring: Deadline with 3s cleanup buffer.\n- Memory: Careful allocation with sparse matrices if needed (but FFT is used often).\n- Robustness measures:\n   - Time limits for each LP/gradient run.\n   - Fallback to parent if errors occur.\n   - Only consider sequences with sum\u22650.01.\n   - Restart with new seed if stalls\u56b4\u91cd.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Always evaluate candidates with `evaluate_sequence`.\n- Return best found sequence (finite score).\n- Validate final sequence against evaluator.\n- Exit if budget exhausted before improvements.",
